    'audit.s1.note' => 'This document is produced by an analysis tool. It reports verifiable facts and the opinion that follows from them; it carries no legal characterisation, and the auditor who signs it remains its author.',
    'audit.s2.p1' => 'Static reading of the repository: cryptographic calls whose algorithm is written out, key material and certificates present in the tree, server and framework configuration, deployment scripts, declared dependencies.',
    'audit.s2.p2' => 'Network probe: an ordinary TLS handshake, the same one a browser performs, against the declared hosts. It establishes what is actually negotiated, which the repository can contradict in either direction.',
    'audit.s2.p3' => 'The inventory is then crossed with how long each kind of data has to stay confidential. The reasoning is Mosca\'s inequality (2015), simplified:',
    'audit.s2.formula' => 'year of the analysis + declared confidentiality lifetime > expiry of the algorithm ⟹ the data already emitted is exposed, and migrating does not repair it',
    'audit.s2.p4' => 'The analysis is reproducible: the same command on the same state of the repository produces the same digest of findings. The tool does not predict the arrival of a quantum computer; it uses the regulatory deadline as its planning date.',
    'audit.s3.intro' => 'These limits appear in the body of the report rather than in a footnote: an inventory that does not say what it failed to look at is not an inventory.',
    'audit.s3.confidence' => 'Findings of medium confidence are marked as such: there the algorithm comes from a variable or a configuration value, and could not be established by reading alone.',
    'audit.s4.intro' => 'The expiry date used here is a regulatory deadline, not a prediction of cryptographic breakage.',
    'audit.ref.fips' => "ML-KEM, ML-DSA and SLH-DSA standardised (August 2024).",
    'audit.ref.nist8547' => "Initial public draft, November 2024. Table 2: RSA and ECDSA at 112 bits of security are deprecated after 2030; RSA, ECDSA and EdDSA are disallowed after 2035 at every level.",
    'audit.ref.cnsa' => "Deadlines by use: software and firmware signing and networking equipment in 2030, browsers, cloud services and operating systems in 2033.",
    'audit.ref.eu' => "Recommendation of 11 April 2024: coordinated national roadmaps, published within two years, and hybrid schemes.",
    'audit.ref.roadmap' => 'Coordinated Implementation Roadmap of the NIS Cooperation Group, 23 June 2025. Classifies use cases by the confidentiality owed: high risk if compromising confidentiality after ten years or more would still cause significant damage. Quantum-vulnerable public-key mechanisms shall not be used stand-alone after the end of 2030 for high risk, after the end of 2035 for medium risk. The cryptographic inventory is one of the First Steps due by 31/12/2026, and CBOM is the format it recommends.',
    'audit.ref.anssi' => "Hybridation held essential wherever post-quantum cryptography is deployed, and required for French security visas. After 2030, ANSSI considers it no longer reasonable to buy a product without post-quantum cryptography.",
    'audit.s4.checked' => 'Deadlines verified against their sources on %s.',
    'audit.s4.retained' => 'Expiry used for this report: %d.',
    'audit.s4.retained.graded' => 'Deadlines retained for this report: %d for high risk and %d for medium risk, the level being read off each domain\'s declared lifetime.',
    'audit.s5.intro' => 'Each row is a direct observation, numbered so that it can be cited. It carries no judgement: the opinion is in section 7.',
    'audit.col.n' => 'No.',
