    'audit.s6.intro' => 'These lifetimes are an input supplied by the party commissioning the audit. The tool can neither verify them nor derive them from the code: the whole of section 7 depends on them, and a corrected lifetime changes the verdict.',
    'audit.s6.default' => 'Lifetime applied to undeclared domains: %d years.',
    'audit.s6.graded' => 'The risk level is computed from the declared lifetime: %d years or more gives high risk, as does a trust anchor that signs software. The roadmap\'s test is whether a compromise after that duration would still cause significant damage; that judgement belongs to whoever declared the lifetime, not to the tool.',
    'audit.s6.none' => 'No declaration was supplied: every lifetime is the default one, and the verdicts below must be read as approximations.',
    'audit.s7.intro' => 'The opinion follows from the findings in section 5 and the lifetimes in section 6. It separates what can be harvested — encryption, which an adversary can capture today and decrypt later — from what cannot be, namely signatures.',
    'audit.s7.concerns' => 'Findings concerned:',
    'audit.s7.references' => 'Published defects, verifiable at NIST:',
    'audit.s9.intro' => 'The seven terms needed to read this report.',
    'audit.g.harvest' => 'Harvest now, decrypt later',
    'audit.g.harvest.def' => 'Capturing encrypted data today in order to decrypt it when a machine able to do so exists. For that data, the date of compromise is the day it was encrypted, not the day of the attack.',
    'audit.g.confidentiality' => 'Confidentiality',
    'audit.g.confidentiality.def' => 'What encryption protects: the data stays unreadable. It is the only property exposed to harvesting.',
    'audit.g.authenticity' => 'Authenticity',
    'audit.g.authenticity.def' => 'What a signature protects: the origin is established. A signature cannot be captured now and forged later; the urgency concerns only long-lived trust anchors.',
    'audit.g.lifetime' => 'Confidentiality lifetime',
    'audit.g.lifetime.def' => 'The number of years a kind of data has to stay secret. It is declared by the commissioning party; no tool can derive it from the code.',
    'audit.g.expiry' => 'Expiry',
    'audit.g.expiry.def' => 'The year from which an algorithm must no longer protect confidential data. Taken here from the texts cited in section 4.',
    'audit.g.symmetric' => 'Symmetric / asymmetric',
    'audit.g.symmetric.def' => 'Symmetric cryptography (AES, ChaCha20) is not the subject: a quantum computer narrows its margin without breaking it. The problem is asymmetric cryptography: RSA, elliptic curves, key exchange.',
    'audit.g.hybrid' => 'Hybridisation',
    'audit.g.hybrid.def' => 'Combining a classical algorithm with a post-quantum one, so that the protection holds if either falls. It is the position of the authorities during the transition.',
    'audit.s10.digest' => 'The digest covers the findings, not this file: two renderings of the same inventory, in two languages, give the same value.',
    'audit.s10.previous' => 'previous report',
