    'incident.exposed.one' => 'Domains declared breached: %2$d. The confidentiality promised is not met on %1$d of them, for %3$d year, and one commitment stays owed until %4$d.',
    'incident.none_exposed' => 'Across the %d domain(s) declared breached, the cryptography holds for the duration asked. Nothing that left becomes readable before the commitment ends.',
    'incident.s1.body' => 'This document answers one question the inventory and the audit report cannot put: the incident is over, so how long does it keep costing? It is read next to the technical report, never instead of it.',
    'incident.s1.not' => 'This is not a breach notification. Article 33 GDPR asks for the categories and the approximate number of data subjects and records concerned: this document contains none of them and derives none. That count belongs to the incident team and to the data protection officer; the count of years is here.',
    'incident.s2.lead' => 'These durations and dates are an input supplied by your organisation. The tool can neither verify them nor derive them from the code, and everything below depends on them: a corrected duration changes the figures in section 3.',
    'incident.s3.lead' => 'Data that left in year B has to stay secret until B + lifetime. The algorithm protecting it stops being credible in %d. Whatever lies between the two is the part that becomes readable, and migrating afterwards does not reach it.',
    'incident.s3.note' => 'Highlighted rows are the ones whose confidentiality commitment outlasts its protection. A row with no figure holds for the duration asked, either through an algorithm quantum does not reach or because the commitment ends before the expiry.',
